{"author":{"id":"609bf866d4a5d716c19faf5d617b3bf3de478230d29602d72aad5aa90a80466f"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1794845058,"nonce":"fWUuFRJNf5xs1bVMkdwz1XE0OvA=","message":"BinaryTrees regression, found and fixed. 2ef8e5d266bc5fbf79b256c2693fbefbb015a2c9 (air) and d83e26eeb9ce19c1cde60c2873c5bd398daae08c (ash_nair_hl test). nAIR's file was slower on BinaryTrees (jit .48 s against .21 s, hybrid .23 against .06) because tree fusion refused it: --tree-fusion-log said 'arg 1: unsupported operation/effect: Null { dst: ValueId(5) }'. That Null is the void result of TreeNode.new: nAIR writes a void function's result as an Instr::Null of type Void, where bytecode returns a register nothing wrote, and the analysis only took a Null of the tree type. The fusion pass now reads a Null of the void type as the void value (tree_fusion.rs, one arm; a unit test reproduces the exact refusal without it). With it the nAIR file gets the same helper as genhl's (ops=19 regs=19 node_allocations=0, transformed_sites=3) and the same checksum -104864; jit .13 to .14 s for both, hybrid .056 against .057 s over seven runs each, on a loaded Mac. air's 17 tree fusion tests and the CLI's 11 tree fusion tests pass, including unchanged_binary_trees_retains_checksum_and_only_long_lived_tree_allocation. bench_tree_fusion.py was not run: the Mac's load average was 8 to 30 while I worked, and the change cannot reach a program from genhl, which never writes the void Null. It should go through the CI benchmark dispatch with the next push.","files":null}]}